WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.780
Inject a rat with radioactive estradiol, and almost every tissue lets the hormone go

2
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.040
again. The uterus and the vagina keep it.

3
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.640
Estradiol is a steroid, and a steroid dissolves in fat.

4
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.720
So it crosses the cell membrane on its own, and nothing on the outer surface has to

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.340
receive it. What inside the cell is built to catch it?

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:32.833
The protein that catches the hormone is a receptor, and it is one chain with two

7
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:38.673
grips. One region closes around the hormone, and another takes hold of a specific

8
00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:40.253
short stretch of DNA.

9
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.793
No second messenger is made, and no kinase cascade runs.

10
00:00:45.813 --> 00:00:51.193
A transcription factor is a protein that binds DNA and controls whether a gene is

11
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:54.393
copied into RNA. The receptor is one.

12
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:02.727
The moving part is helix 12, the last helix of the hormone-binding domain.

13
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:07.367
An agonist is a molecule that switches the receptor on.

14
00:01:08.227 --> 00:01:13.667
With one in the pocket, helix 12 folds across the mouth like a lid, and that

15
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.367
completes a shallow groove.

16
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:23.947
A helper protein called a co-activator carries a short helix, LXXLL, that fits the

17
00:01:23.947 --> 00:01:27.687
groove. The groove is the hormone's whole message.

18
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:34.827
DNA in the nucleus is wound around proteins called histones.

19
00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:39.267
Tightly packed DNA is hard to copy into RNA.

20
00:01:40.227 --> 00:01:44.727
A co-activator brings enzymes that add acetyl groups to histones.

21
00:01:45.387 --> 00:01:49.367
That loosens the packing and helps RNA polymerase start.

22
00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:52.027
Then the gene can switch on.

23
00:01:53.917 --> 00:01:59.857
An antagonist is a molecule that occupies the receptor without switching it on.

24
00:02:00.957 --> 00:02:06.237
The liver converts tamoxifen into forms that bind the receptor much more tightly,

25
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:09.237
such as 4-hydroxytamoxifen.

26
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:15.917
That molecule fits the pocket, but one side chain is too long to stay inside.

27
00:02:16.457 --> 00:02:18.717
It sticks out where the lid should close.

28
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:25.897
Instead, helix 12 lies in the groove itself, so the co-activator has nowhere to

29
00:02:25.897 --> 00:02:26.897
bind.

30
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:34.307
Tamoxifen blocks the action of estrogen in the breast, yet it imitates estrogen in

31
00:02:34.307 --> 00:02:35.767
bone and in the uterus.

32
00:02:36.707 --> 00:02:40.907
The receptor with tamoxifen in its pocket still binds DNA.

33
00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:46.187
What happens next could depend on the helper proteins each tissue makes.

34
00:02:47.027 --> 00:02:53.747
Keep this rule: where helix 12 lies decides whether the groove is free for a co-activator.
